start.js (excerpt)<pre><code>process.env.USER_CONFIG_PATH = userConfigPath;
// Let Next.js middleware reach FastAPI over the loopback interface inside the
// container without having to bounce through nginx (the host-facing port is
// not reachable from inside the Next.js process).
if (!process.env.FAST_API_INTERNAL_URL) {
  process.env.FAST_API_INTERNAL_URL = `http://127.0.0.1:${fastapiPort}`;
}
</code></pre> Variable Value Consumers <code>USER_CONFIG_PATH</code> <code>/app_data/userConfig.json</code> FastAPI <code>utils/get_env.py:63-64</code> (provider settings, auth secret), Next.js <code>lib/fastapi-internal.ts:27</code>, user-config route handlers <code>FAST_API_INTERNAL_URL</code> <code>http://127.0.0.1:8000</code> unless already set Next.js <code>proxy.ts:10-23</code>, <code>utils/serverAuth.ts:17-30</code>, <code>lib/fastapi-internal.ts:3-13</code> <code>HOSTNAME</code>, <code>PORT</code> <code>127.0.0.1</code>, <code>3000</code> only for the standalone Next.js server (<code>start.js:557-562</code>) <p>Every child is spawned with <code>env: process.env</code>, so all container environment variables (provider keys, <code>PRESENTATION_GENERATION_MODE</code>, <code>DATABASE_URL</code> and the rest) reach FastAPI, MCP and Next.js unchanged.</p>

start.js (excerpt)<pre><code>const main = async () =&gt; {
  await ensurePresentationExportRuntime();
  await ensureOllamaRuntime();

  if (isDev) {
    await setupNodeModules();
    await ensurePresentationExportNodeDependencies();
  }

  setupUserConfigFromEnv();

  syncNginxConfigForDev();

  const nginxReadyPromise = startNginx();
  startServers(nginxReadyPromise);
  await nginxReadyPromise;
};
</code></pre>

start.js (excerpt)<pre><code>const setupUserConfigFromEnv = () =&gt; {
  const existingConfig = readUserConfig();
  const envConfig = readUserConfigEnv(process.env);
  if (Object.keys(existingConfig).length &gt; 0 &amp;&amp; Object.keys(envConfig).length === 0) {
    return;
  }
  writeUserConfig(buildUserConfigFromEnv(existingConfig, process.env));
};
</code></pre> <p><code>readUserConfig()</code> falls back to <code>userConfig.json.bak</code> when the main file is missing or corrupt (<code>start.js:126-127</code>). <code>readUserConfigEnv</code> picks only the keys in <code>USER_CONFIG_ENV_KEYS</code> (<code>scripts/user-config-env.cjs:20-116</code>), ignoring empty strings. <code>buildUserConfigFromEnv</code> merges env over the existing file, converts boolean-like and integer values, and picks a default <code>IMAGE_PROVIDER</code> when none is given (<code>scripts/user-config-env.cjs:186-222</code>). <code>writeUserConfig</code> is crash-safe: it copies the current file to <code>.bak</code>, writes a unique temp file opened with <code>wx</code> and mode <code>0600</code>, <code>fsync</code>s it, then <code>rename</code>s it over the target (<code>start.js:140-168</code>). The details of the merge and how FastAPI consumes it belong to Configuration and LLM providers.</p> <p>Gotcha</p> <p>On the first FastAPI startup, <code>migrate_provider_settings_from_file</code> copies <code>userConfig.json</code> into a <code>ProviderSettings</code> database row. On every later startup the database row is mirrored back over the file, keeping only <code>AUTH_*</code> keys from the file (<code>servers/fastapi/services/provider_settings.py:85-127</code>). So editing provider variables in <code>docker-compose.yml</code> after the first boot does not reliably change the active provider: <code>start.js</code> writes them, then FastAPI's lifespan replaces them with the stored settings. Change providers in the Settings UI instead.</p>

start.js (excerpt)<pre><code>const nextjsReadyPromise = waitForProcessOutputLine("Next.js", nextjsProcess, [
  /Ready in\s+\d+/i,
  /started server on/i,
]);
const fastApiReadyPromise = isDev
  ? waitForProcessOutputLine("FastAPI", fastApiProcess, [
      /Application startup complete\./i,
    ])
  : waitForProcessHttp(
      "FastAPI",
      fastApiProcess,
      fastapiPort,
      "/api/v1/auth/status"
    );
</code></pre> <ul> <li>Next.js readiness (both modes) is a regex match on any stdout or stderr line (<code>start.js:269-330</code>).</li> <li>FastAPI readiness in production is an HTTP probe: <code>GET http://127.0.0.1:8000/api/v1/auth/status</code>, 1 second per attempt, retried every 250 ms, giving up after 60 seconds (<code>start.js:332-416</code>). Any HTTP response counts as ready, whatever its status code (<code>start.js:373-376</code>). <code>/api/v1/auth/status</code> is listed in <code>SessionAuthMiddleware._PUBLIC_AUTH_PATHS</code> (<code>servers/fastapi/api/middlewares.py:30-36</code>), so it answers before any admin exists.</li> <li>In dev, FastAPI readiness is uvicorn's <code>Application startup complete.</code> line, which appears only after the lifespan (migrations, template import) finishes.</li> </ul> <p><code>Promise.all([fastApi, nextjs, nginx])</code> gates only the startup banner (<code>start.js:681-690</code>). On failure it logs <code>Skipping startup banner: ...</code>. Readiness never kills anything.</p> <p>In production <code>forwardProcessOutput(..., { suppressStartupUrls: true })</code> hides lines such as <code>Uvicorn running on http://127.0.0.1:8000</code> (<code>start.js:229-234</code>, <code>644-656</code>) so users are not misled into opening internal loopback ports; the banner prints the public URL instead.</p>

start.js (excerpt)<pre><code>childProcess.on("exit", (code, signal) =&gt; {
  managedProcesses.delete(childProcess);
  if (isShuttingDown) {
    return;
  }

  const exitCode = typeof code === "number" ? code : signal ? 1 : 0;
  if (isDev &amp;&amp; exitCode === 0 &amp;&amp; restart) {
    console.warn(`${name} exited cleanly; restarting it for development.`);
    setTimeout(() =&gt; {
      if (!isShuttingDown) {
        restart();
      }
    }, 1000);
    return;
  }
  // ...
  shutdown(exitCode);
});
</code></pre> Situation Behavior Production, FastAPI/Next.js/Ollama exits (any code) <code>shutdown(exitCode)</code>: kill every managed child, <code>process.exit(exitCode)</code>; the container stops Dev, FastAPI or Next.js exits with code 0 restart after 1 s; the new child gets <code>stdio: ["ignore", "inherit", "inherit"]</code> (<code>start.js:602-605</code>, <code>623-626</code>) Dev, non-zero exit or killed by a signal same as production: whole container exits Ollama exits (any mode) no restart function is passed (<code>start.js:670</code>), so <code>shutdown</code> MCP server exits nothing happens; the process is not watched <code>SIGINT</code> / <code>SIGTERM</code> to Node <code>shutdown(0)</code> (<code>start.js:518-519</code>) <p>Gotcha</p> <p>The MCP server is spawned with <code>stdio: "ignore"</code> and no exit watcher (<code>start.js:608-620</code>). If <code>mcp_server.py</code> crashes on startup (for example because <code>openai_spec.json</code> is missing or malformed), <code>/mcp</code> returns nginx 502 forever and there are no logs anywhere. Run <code>python mcp_server.py --port 8001</code> by hand inside the container to see its <code>DEBUG:</code> and <code>ERROR:</code> output.</p> <p>After <code>startServers</code> returns its spawn work it awaits a never-resolving promise (<code>start.js:692</code>), which keeps the Node event loop alive.</p>

servers/fastapi/api/middlewares.py (excerpt)<pre><code>    def _requires_auth(self, path: str) -&gt; bool:
        if any(path.startswith(prefix) for prefix in self._PUBLIC_AUTH_PREFIXES):
            return False
        if path.startswith("/api/"):
            return True
        if any(path.startswith(prefix) for prefix in self._PUBLIC_APP_DATA_PREFIXES):
            return False
        if path.startswith("/app_data/"):
            return True
        return path in self._PROTECTED_NON_API_PATHS
</code></pre> Path Needs auth <code>/api/v1/auth/status</code>, <code>/verify</code>, <code>/setup</code>, <code>/login</code>, <code>/logout</code> (<code>_PUBLIC_AUTH_PATHS</code>, <code>middlewares.py:30</code>) no any other <code>/api/...</code> (including <code>/api/v1/auth/presenton/...</code>, because <code>_PUBLIC_AUTH_PREFIXES</code> is empty) yes <code>/app_data/fonts/...</code>, <code>/app_data/templates/...</code> no any other <code>/app_data/...</code> yes <code>/docs</code>, <code>/openapi.json</code>, <code>/redoc</code> yes everything else, including <code>/static/...</code> no <p><code>OPTIONS</code> requests always pass, so CORS preflights reach <code>CORSMiddleware</code>.</p>

servers/fastapi/api/middlewares.py (excerpt)<pre><code>            admin_only = (
                path.startswith("/api/v1/admin/")
                or path.startswith("/api/v1/ppt/codex/auth/")
                or (
                    path.startswith("/api/v1/ppt/fonts/")
                    and request.method in {"POST", "DELETE"}
                )
                or path in {
                    "/api/v1/ppt/ollama/models/pull",
                    "/api/v1/ppt/ollama/models/available",
                    "/api/v1/ppt/openai/models/available",
                    "/api/v1/ppt/images/test",
                    "/api/v1/ppt/openrouter/providers/available",
                }
            )
            if admin_only and (principal.method != "jwt" or not principal.is_admin):
                return JSONResponse(
                    status_code=403,
                    content={"detail": "Admin browser session required"},
                )
</code></pre> <p>These are the endpoints that change global state (users, API keys, provider settings, the global ChatGPT token, shared fonts) or that make the server fetch an arbitrary URL supplied by the caller (OpenAI-compatible and Ollama model discovery, image provider tests).</p> <p>Gotcha</p> <p>The check requires <code>principal.method == "jwt"</code>. An API key that belongs to the administrator still cannot reach these paths, by design: API keys are for automation, and settings changes require a browser session. Note also that <code>/api/v1/ppt/anthropic/models/available</code> and <code>/api/v1/ppt/google/models/available</code> are not in the list; they call fixed vendor hosts with the caller's key, so any signed-in user may use them.</p>

servers/fastapi/api/middlewares.py (excerpt)<pre><code>            context_token = set_current_owner_id(principal.user_id)
            admin_context_token = set_current_owner_is_admin(principal.is_admin)
            try:
                if principal.method == "jwt":
                    cloud_response = await maybe_proxy_presenton_cloud_request(
                        request,
                        session,
                        user,
                    )
                    if cloud_response is not None:
                        return cloud_response
                if path.startswith(
                    "/app_data/"
                ) and not is_app_data_path_authorized(
                    path,
                    user_id=principal.user_id,
                    is_admin=principal.is_admin,
                ):
                    return JSONResponse(
                        status_code=404,
                        content={"detail": "Asset not found"},
                    )
                return await call_next(request)
            finally:
                reset_current_owner_is_admin(admin_context_token)
                reset_current_owner_id(context_token)
</code></pre> <ul> <li>Presenton Cloud proxy. For browser sessions, <code>maybe_proxy_presenton_cloud_request</code> (<code>services/presenton_cloud_proxy.py:407</code>) forwards a fixed set of generation, editing, chat and asset paths to Presenton Cloud when the saved provider is <code>LLM = "presenton"</code>, then mirrors results into the local database. It is described in Configuration and LLM providers.</li> <li><code>/app_data</code> authorization. <code>is_app_data_path_authorized</code> (<code>assets.py:78</code>) decodes the URL path up to 8 times (failing closed beyond that), rejects NUL bytes, backslashes and <code>.</code>/<code>..</code> segments, applies the namespace rule from Step 4, and then re-applies it to the <code>realpath</code> of the target so a symlink cannot alias another user's file. A refusal returns 404, not 403, so the response does not reveal that the file exists.</li> </ul>

Consumer Effect <code>_scope_owned_selects</code>, a <code>do_orm_execute</code> listener on every <code>Session</code> (<code>services/database.py:67</code>) adds <code>with_loader_criteria(model, owner_id == current)</code> for seven tables, and "own or shared default" for <code>TemplateV2</code>, to every ORM <code>SELECT</code> <code>_stamp_new_owned_rows</code>, a <code>before_flush</code> listener (<code>services/database.py:100</code>) sets <code>owner_id = current owner</code> on every new owned object before INSERT <code>owner_id</code> fields with <code>default_factory=get_current_owner_id</code> in eight SQL models (for example <code>models/sql/presentation.py:25</code>) fills <code>owner_id</code> the moment the Python object is constructed <code>_owned_directory</code> (<code>utils/asset_directory_utils.py:23</code>) writes files under <code>&lt;root&gt;/users/&lt;owner_id&gt;/</code> <code>_OwnerScopedStaticFiles.lookup_path</code> (<code>assets.py:25</code>) refuses other users' files <code>authenticated_internal_request_headers</code> (<code>api/v1/auth/internal.py:10</code>) mints a JWT cookie for server-to-server calls on behalf of the owner servers/fastapi/services/database.py (excerpt)<pre><code>@event.listens_for(Session, "do_orm_execute")
def _scope_owned_selects(execute_state) -&gt; None:
    """Apply tenant criteria to every ORM SELECT performed during a request."""
    owner_id = get_current_owner_id()
    if (
        owner_id is None
        or not execute_state.is_select
        or execute_state.execution_options.get("skip_owner_scope")
    ):
        return

    statement = execute_state.statement
    for model in _STRICT_OWNER_MODELS:
        statement = statement.options(
            with_loader_criteria(
                model,
                lambda row: row.owner_id == owner_id,
                include_aliases=True,
            )
        )
</code></pre>

servers/fastapi/utils/db_utils.py (excerpt)<pre><code>def get_database_url_and_connect_args() -&gt; tuple[str, dict]:
    database_url = get_database_url_env() or "sqlite:///" + os.path.join(
        get_app_data_directory_env() or "/tmp/presenton", "fastapi.db"
    )

    _ensure_sqlite_parent_dir(database_url)

    if database_url.startswith("sqlite://"):
        database_url = database_url.replace("sqlite://", "sqlite+aiosqlite://", 1)
    elif database_url.startswith("postgresql://"):
        database_url = database_url.replace("postgresql://", "postgresql+asyncpg://", 1)
    elif database_url.startswith("mysql://"):
        database_url = database_url.replace("mysql://", "mysql+aiomysql://", 1)
    else:
        database_url = database_url
</code></pre> <p>The resolution order is:</p> <ol> <li><code>DATABASE_URL</code> (<code>get_database_url_env</code>, <code>servers/fastapi/utils/get_env.py:40</code>) if it is set and non-empty.</li> <li>Otherwise <code>sqlite:///&lt;APP_DATA_DIRECTORY&gt;/fastapi.db</code> (<code>get_app_data_directory_env</code>, <code>servers/fastapi/utils/get_env.py:44</code>). In Docker, <code>APP_DATA_DIRECTORY=/app_data</code> (<code>Dockerfile:82</code>), so the file is <code>/app_data/fastapi.db</code>.</li> <li>If <code>APP_DATA_DIRECTORY</code> is unset too, <code>/tmp/presenton/fastapi.db</code>.</li> </ol> <p><code>_ensure_sqlite_parent_dir</code> (<code>servers/fastapi/utils/db_utils.py:7</code>) creates the parent folder of a SQLite file with <code>os.makedirs(..., exist_ok=True)</code>, and strips the leading slash of Windows paths such as <code>/C:/...</code> before doing so.</p>

servers/fastapi/utils/llm_calls/generate_slide_content.py (excerpt)<pre><code>def _prepare_response_schema(json_schema: Optional[dict]) -&gt; Optional[dict]:
    if not isinstance(json_schema, dict):
        return None

    response_schema = remove_fields_from_schema(json_schema, ASSET_ONLY_FIELDS)
    if not _schema_has_content_fields(response_schema):
        return None

    if response_schema.get("type") != "object":
        response_schema["type"] = "object"

    response_schema = add_field_in_schema(
        response_schema,
        {
            "__speaker_note__": {
                "type": "string",
                "minLength": 100,
                "maxLength": 500,
                "description": "Speaker note for the slide",
            }
        },
        True,
    )
    return ensure_array_schemas_have_items(response_schema)
</code></pre> <p><code>ASSET_ONLY_FIELDS = ["__image_url__", "__icon_url__"]</code> (<code>generate_slide_content.py:86</code>). If the remaining schema has no <code>properties</code>, <code>get_slide_content_from_type_and_outline</code> returns <code>{}</code> without creating a client (<code>generate_slide_content.py:240-242</code>); <code>test_slide_content_generation_skips_asset_only_schema</code> (<code>servers/fastapi/tests/unit/test_generate_slide_content.py:55</code>) covers this.</p>

servers/fastapi/utils/llm_calls/generate_slide_content.py (excerpt)<pre><code>SLIDE_CONTENT_USER_PROMPT = """
# Current Date and Time:
{current_date_time}

# Icon Query And Image Prompt Language:
English

# Slide Language:
{language}

{slide_number_section}
# SLIDE CONTENT: START
{content}
# SLIDE CONTENT: END
"""
</code></pre> <p><code>{language}</code> is the stored language, or, when empty or <code>auto</code>, the instruction "auto-detect from the slide content and use the same language as the slide content" (<code>generate_slide_content.py:87-100</code>). In the web UI path <code>/prepare</code> clears the language, so this instruction is what slides always see. <code>{slide_number_section}</code> is <code># Slide Number:\n&lt;n&gt;</code> with a one-based number (<code>generate_slide_content.py:152-155</code>), which lets "on slide 5" instructions apply. <code>{content}</code> is the outline Markdown for this slide.</p>

Order <code>type</code> Payload Source 1 <code>chunk</code> <code>"{ \"slides\": [ "</code> (opening text) <code>presentation.py:2360-2363</code> per slide <code>chunk</code> the slide as compact JSON (<code>slide.model_dump_json()</code>) <code>presentation.py:2421-2424</code> interleaved <code>slide_assets</code> <code>{"type": "slide_assets", "slide_index": i, "slide": {...}, "warnings": [...]}</code> <code>presentation.py:2426-2446</code>, <code>presentation.py:2453-2470</code> after last slide <code>chunk</code> <code>" ] }"</code> (closing text) <code>presentation.py:2448-2451</code> remaining <code>slide_assets</code> one per slide whose assets were not yet reported <code>presentation.py:2453-2470</code> last (success) <code>complete</code> <code>{"type": "complete", "presentation": &lt;PresentationWithSlides&gt;}</code> <code>presentation.py:2503-2511</code> last (failure) <code>error</code> <code>{"type": "error", "detail": "..."}</code> <code>presentation.py:2379-2381</code> or <code>safe_sse_stream</code> <p>Exactly one <code>slide_assets</code> frame is sent per slide, either right after a later slide's <code>chunk</code> (if the assets finished while the next LLM call was running) or after the closing chunk. A slide frame looks like this (abridged; field order follows <code>SlideModel</code>, <code>servers/fastapi/models/sql/slide.py:9-30</code>, and <code>owner_id</code> is excluded):</p> <pre><code>event: response
data: {"type": "chunk", "chunk": "{\"id\":\"5c1e...\",\"presentation\":\"8f0c...\",\"layout_group\":\"3b6f...\",\"layout\":\"intro-hero\",\"index\":0,\"content\":{\"hero\":{\"headline\":\"Q3 Business Review\"},\"__speaker_note__\":\"...\"},\"html_content\":null,\"speaker_note\":\"...\",\"properties\":null,\"ui\":{...}}"}

event: response
data: {"type": "slide_assets", "slide_index": 0, "slide": {"id": "5c1e...", "index": 0, "content": {"hero": {"headline": "Q3 Business Review"}, "hero_image": {"image_prompt": "...", "image_url": "/app_data/images/..."}}, "ui": {...}}, "warnings": []}
</code></pre> <p>Warning objects are either image-provider warnings <code>{"status_code": 502, "detail": "...", "code": null}</code> from <code>image_generation_warning</code> (<code>servers/fastapi/utils/image_generation_error.py:75-86</code>) or <code>{"type": "asset_generation_failed", "message": "Some slide assets could not be generated."}</code> when the whole asset task raised (<code>presentation.py:2350-2355</code>).</p> <p>Gotcha</p> <p>The <code>chunk</code> frames concatenate to <code>{ "slides": [ {slide0}{slide1} ] }</code>: there is no comma between slides, so the accumulated text is not valid JSON. The browser copes because it parses each slide chunk on its own and runs <code>jsonrepair</code> over the accumulation (<code>usePresentationStreaming.ts:454-510</code>). If you write a new client, parse each slide chunk individually rather than the concatenation.</p>

servers/fastapi/services/image_generation_service.py (excerpt)<pre><code>class ImageGenerationService:
    def __init__(self, output_directory: str, config: dict[str, str] | None = None):
        self.output_directory = output_directory
        self.config = config
        self.is_image_generation_disabled = (
            False if config is not None else is_image_generation_disabled()
        )
        self.image_gen_func = self.get_image_gen_func()

    def _setting(self, key: str, getter):
        # Empty form values must not fall back to a different saved configuration.
        return self.config.get(key) if self.config is not None else getter()
</code></pre> <p>There are two modes:</p> <ul> <li>Environment mode (<code>config=None</code>): used by every real generation path. <code>get_image_gen_func</code> walks an <code>if/elif</code> chain of <code>is_*_selected()</code> helpers (<code>servers/fastapi/services/image_generation_service.py:162</code>).</li> <li>Explicit config mode (<code>config={...}</code>): used only by <code>POST /api/v1/ppt/images/test</code>. The provider is looked up by <code>config["IMAGE_PROVIDER"]</code> in a dict (<code>servers/fastapi/services/image_generation_service.py:147</code>), every setting comes from that dict, and <code>DISABLE_IMAGE_GENERATION</code> is ignored.</li> </ul> <p><code>is_stock_provider_selected</code> (<code>servers/fastapi/services/image_generation_service.py:182</code>) is true for <code>pexels</code> and <code>pixabay</code>. That flag changes both the prompt (Step 3) and the call signature (stock functions receive only the query string).</p>

servers/fastapi/services/image_generation_service.py (excerpt)<pre><code>def _closest_ratio_option(
    target_size: tuple[float, float] | None,
    options: tuple[str, ...],
    separator: str,
) -&gt; str | None:
    if target_size is None:
        return None
    target_ratio = target_size[0] / target_size[1]

    def ratio(option: str) -&gt; float:
        width, height = option.split(separator)
        return float(width) / float(height)

    return min(
        options,
        key=lambda option: abs(math.log(target_ratio / ratio(option))),
    )
</code></pre> <p>Comparing ratios in log space makes "twice as wide" and "twice as tall" equally far from square. The option lists are:</p> <ul> <li>OpenAI (<code>GPT_IMAGE_SIZES</code>, <code>servers/fastapi/services/image_generation_service.py:55</code>): <code>1024x1024</code>, <code>1536x1024</code>, <code>1024x1536</code>. Used only when the model name is <code>gpt-image-2</code> or <code>gpt-image-1.5</code>; any other model gets <code>1024x1024</code> (<code>_openai_image_size</code>, <code>servers/fastapi/services/image_generation_service.py:93</code>).</li> <li>Gemini (<code>GEMINI_IMAGE_RATIOS</code>, <code>servers/fastapi/services/image_generation_service.py:56</code>): <code>1:1</code>, <code>2:3</code>, <code>3:2</code>, <code>3:4</code>, <code>4:3</code>, <code>4:5</code>, <code>5:4</code>, <code>9:16</code>, <code>16:9</code>, <code>21:9</code>. With no target size, no <code>image_config</code> is sent and the model picks.</li> <li>ComfyUI: no mapping; the rounded pixel dimensions are written into the workflow (Step 5.4).</li> <li>Open WebUI: always <code>1024x1024</code>, target size ignored (<code>servers/fastapi/services/image_generation_service.py:345</code>).</li> </ul> <p>A worked example: a full-bleed 1280 by 720 box has ratio 1.778. Against <code>1536x1024</code> (ratio 1.5) the distance is <code>|ln(1.778 / 1.5)| = 0.170</code>; against <code>1024x1024</code> it is <code>0.575</code>; against <code>1024x1536</code> it is <code>0.981</code>. The landscape size wins. The unit tests pin a few more cases (<code>servers/fastapi/tests/unit/test_template_image_targets.py:166</code> and <code>servers/fastapi/tests/unit/test_template_image_targets.py:183</code>):</p> Target (w, h) OpenAI size Gemini ratio none <code>1024x1024</code> none (auto) (1600, 900) <code>1536x1024</code> <code>16:9</code> (900, 1600) <code>1024x1536</code> <code>9:16</code> (1200, 1100) <code>1024x1024</code> not tested (1000, 1000) not tested <code>1:1</code> (500, 400) not tested <code>5:4</code> (2100, 900) not tested <code>21:9</code> <p>Note</p> <p>Presenton never resizes or crops the returned image to the slot. A 1024 by 1024 PNG stays 1024 by 1024 on disk (<code>servers/fastapi/tests/unit/test_template_image_targets.py:300</code>). Fitting is done by the renderer; see From template to schema to slide.</p>

