servers/fastapi/utils/export_utils.py (excerpt)<pre><code>def _build_presentation_export_url(
    presentation_id: uuid.UUID, cookie_header: str | None = None
) -&gt; tuple[str, str | None]:
    params = {"id": str(presentation_id)}
    fastapi_url = _get_next_public_fastapi_url()
    if fastapi_url:
        params["fastapiUrl"] = fastapi_url
    export_url = f"{_get_next_public_url().rstrip('/')}/pdf-maker?{urlencode(params)}"
    if cookie_header:
        export_url = f"{export_url}#{urlencode({'exportCookie': cookie_header})}"
    return (
        export_url,
        fastapi_url,
    )
</code></pre> <p>Compared with the Next.js path: no <code>format</code> and no <code>exportSession</code> in the query; the cookie travels only in the fragment and in <code>cookieHeader</code>. <code>_get_next_public_url()</code> defaults to <code>http://127.0.0.1</code> (<code>servers/fastapi/utils/export_utils.py:18-19</code>). In the Docker image that is nginx on port 80 inside the container, which proxies <code>/pdf-maker</code> to Next.js on port 3000 (<code>nginx.conf:29-43</code>).</p> <p>Gotcha: running FastAPI outside Docker</p> <p>If you run FastAPI and Next.js directly on your machine (see Running it locally), nothing listens on <code>http://127.0.0.1:80</code>, and every API export fails inside the headless browser. Set <code>NEXT_PUBLIC_URL</code> (for example <code>http://127.0.0.1:3000</code>) in FastAPI's environment.</p> <p>The file name is computed as <code>safe_export_basename(sanitize_filename(name))</code>, where <code>name</code> is the trimmed title or a random UUID (<code>servers/fastapi/utils/export_utils.py:58-62</code>). <code>sanitize_filename</code> comes from the <code>pathvalidate</code> package; <code>safe_export_basename()</code> (<code>servers/fastapi/utils/filename_utils.py:6-18</code>) keeps names up to 200 UTF-8 bytes unchanged and otherwise truncates to 191 bytes (on a character boundary) plus <code>_</code> plus the first 8 hex characters of the MD5 of the full name. The test <code>servers/fastapi/tests/unit/test_filename_utils.py:22-25</code> asserts that the result plus <code>.pptx</code> fits the 255-byte file name limit. That test only makes sense if export-core uses <code>title</code> as the output file's base name, which is the strongest evidence in this repository for how the engine names its output (the engine itself is not available to confirm it).</p>

servers/fastapi/api/middlewares.py (excerpt)<pre><code>class SessionAuthMiddleware(BaseHTTPMiddleware):
    _PUBLIC_AUTH_PATHS = {
        "/api/v1/auth/status",
        "/api/v1/auth/verify",
        "/api/v1/auth/setup",
        "/api/v1/auth/login",
        "/api/v1/auth/logout",
    }
    _PUBLIC_AUTH_PREFIXES: tuple[str, ...] = ()
    _PUBLIC_APP_DATA_PREFIXES = (
        "/app_data/fonts/",
        "/app_data/templates/",
    )
    _PROTECTED_NON_API_PATHS = {"/docs", "/openapi.json", "/redoc"}

    def _requires_auth(self, path: str) -&gt; bool:
        if any(path.startswith(prefix) for prefix in self._PUBLIC_AUTH_PREFIXES):
            return False
        if path.startswith("/api/"):
            return True
        if any(path.startswith(prefix) for prefix in self._PUBLIC_APP_DATA_PREFIXES):
            return False
        if path.startswith("/app_data/"):
            return True
        return path in self._PROTECTED_NON_API_PATHS
</code></pre> <p>Every <code>/api/*</code> path requires a principal except the five exact auth paths. That includes the Presenton OAuth routes under <code>/api/v1/auth/presenton/</code>, which are not in the public set (tested by <code>test_presenton_provider_endpoints_require_a_local_session</code>, <code>servers/fastapi/tests/unit/test_session_auth_middleware.py:39</code>). <code>/app_data/fonts/</code> and <code>/app_data/templates/</code> are public; every other <code>/app_data/</code> path is protected. The interactive API docs (<code>/docs</code>, <code>/openapi.json</code>, <code>/redoc</code>) require a session too, and everything else (for example <code>/static/...</code>) is public.</p>

servers/fastapi/api/v1/auth/principal.py (excerpt)<pre><code>async def resolve_request_principal(
    request: Request, session: AsyncSession
) -&gt; tuple[AuthPrincipal | None, User | None]:
    cookie_token = request.cookies.get(SESSION_COOKIE_NAME)
    if cookie_token:
        user_db = UsernameUserDatabase(session)
        user = await get_jwt_strategy().read_token(cookie_token, UserManager(user_db))
        if user:
            return (
                AuthPrincipal(
                    user_id=user.id,
                    username=user.username,
                    is_admin=user.is_superuser,
                    method="jwt",
                ),
                user,
            )

    authorization = request.headers.get("Authorization", "")
    if authorization.lower().startswith("bearer "):
        token = authorization.split(" ", 1)[1].strip()
        verified = await verify_api_key(session, token)
        if verified is None:
            return None, None
        # ...
    return None, None
</code></pre> <p>The resolution order is:</p> <ol> <li><code>presenton_session</code> cookie, if present and valid, wins. <code>method = "jwt"</code>.</li> <li>Otherwise an <code>Authorization: Bearer ...</code> header is treated as an API key. <code>method = "api_key"</code>.</li> <li>Otherwise no principal.</li> </ol> <p><code>AuthPrincipal</code> (<code>principal.py:14</code>) is a frozen dataclass of <code>user_id</code>, <code>username</code>, <code>is_admin</code> and <code>method</code>. Note the type annotation: <code>method</code> is <code>Literal["jwt", "api_key"]</code>. There is no third "internal" method. Internal callers (the export browser, background tasks, the MCP server) authenticate by presenting a normal <code>presenton_session</code> JWT that the server minted for them (Step 7).</p> <p>Gotcha: a valid cookie always beats a bearer header</p> <p>If the cookie is present but invalid, the code falls through to the bearer branch, so a client that sends both a stale cookie and a valid API key still authenticates with the key. If the cookie is valid, the bearer header is ignored entirely, even if it names a different user.</p>

