</code></pre> Admin-only path Why it is privileged <code>/api/v1/admin/*</code> Users, API keys, provider settings <code>/api/v1/ppt/codex/auth/*</code> ChatGPT/Codex OAuth credentials for the whole installation <code>POST</code>/<code>DELETE /api/v1/ppt/fonts/*</code> Shared font library mutations Ollama pull and available, OpenAI and OpenRouter discovery, image provider test Outbound requests to admin-supplied URLs (SSRF surface) or shared secrets <p>The rule is "admin AND browser session": an admin's API key gets <code>403</code> too (<code>test_api_keys_cannot_access_browser_admin_routes</code>, <code>servers/fastapi/tests/unit/test_session_auth_middleware.py:162</code>). The helper <code>require_browser_admin_principal</code> (<code>principal.py:67</code>) encodes the same rule for route-level use.</p>

nginx.conf (excerpt)<pre><code>  client_max_body_size 100M;
  # Keep the browser-facing origin supplied by an outer reverse proxy. Direct
  # deployments fall back to this nginx listener's own scheme and Host header.
  map $http_x_forwarded_proto $presenton_forwarded_proto {
    "" $scheme;
    default $http_x_forwarded_proto;
  }
  map $http_x_forwarded_host $presenton_forwarded_host {
    "" $http_host;
    default $http_x_forwarded_host;
  }
</code></pre> Location Upstream Body limit Timeouts Forwarded headers <code>/</code> Next.js <code>:3000</code> 100M read/connect 30m <code>Host $http_host</code>, <code>X-Real-IP</code>, <code>X-Forwarded-For</code>, mapped <code>X-Forwarded-Proto</code>/<code>-Host</code>, WebSocket upgrade <code>/api/v1/</code> FastAPI <code>:8000</code> 110M, <code>413</code> returns <code>{"detail":"File size must be less than 100MB."}</code> read/send/connect 30m same (no upgrade) <code>/api/v2/</code> FastAPI <code>:8000</code> 100M 30m same <code>/mcp</code>, <code>/mcp/</code> MCP <code>:8001</code> 145M 30m <code>Host localhost:8001</code> (FastMCP host allowlist) plus mapped forwarded headers <code>/docs</code>, <code>/openapi.json</code> FastAPI 100M 30m <code>Host $host</code>, <code>X-Forwarded-Proto $scheme</code> <code>/static/</code> <code>alias</code> to <code>/app/servers/fastapi/static/</code> public, <code>Cache-Control: public, immutable</code> <code>/app_data/*</code> <code>alias</code> to <code>/app_data/*</code> see Auth and multi-user <p>The 30-minute timeouts exist for long SSE generation streams and exports. <code>Host $http_host</code> (not <code>$host</code>) keeps the port, which the comment at <code>nginx.conf:34</code> says Next.js needs for redirects and absolute URLs.</p> <p>Downstream, the forwarded headers drive three behaviors: the <code>Secure</code> flag on the session cookie (<code>_secure_request</code>, <code>servers/fastapi/api/v1/auth/router.py:39</code>), MCP result links (<code>absolute_mcp_url</code>, <code>servers/fastapi/utils/mcp_public_urls.py:17</code>) and the <code>/pdf-maker</code> session cookie in <code>servers/nextjs/proxy.ts:126</code>.</p> <p>Gotcha: nginx trusts incoming <code>X-Forwarded-*</code></p> <p>The <code>map</code> blocks pass any client-supplied <code>X-Forwarded-Proto</code> or <code>X-Forwarded-Host</code> through unchanged. When nginx is directly exposed (no outer proxy), a client can set them. Put a proxy in front that overwrites these headers, or set <code>PRESENTON_PUBLIC_URL</code> so generated links do not depend on them.</p>

electron/app/main.ts (excerpt)<pre><code>function resolveElectronDisableAuth(): string {
  const raw = (
    process.env.ELECTRON_DISABLE_AUTH ?? process.env.DISABLE_AUTH
  )?.trim().toLowerCase();
  if (!raw) {
    return "true";
  }
  if (["0", "false", "no", "off"].includes(raw)) {
    return "false";
  }
  if (isDisableAuthEnabledValue(raw)) {
    return "true";
  }
  return "true";
}
</code></pre> <p>Auth is disabled unless someone explicitly sets <code>ELECTRON_DISABLE_AUTH</code> or <code>DISABLE_AUTH</code> to a false value (<code>:150</code> to <code>:164</code>). The result is written back into <code>process.env.DISABLE_AUTH</code>, <code>ELECTRON_DISABLE_AUTH</code>, and <code>PRESENTON_ELECTRON=true</code> (<code>:456</code> to <code>:459</code>). With auth disabled, FastAPI's <code>SessionAuthMiddleware</code> skips account checks (see Auth and multi-user), and rows are created with a null <code>owner_id</code>.</p>

Area Requirement Target or limit Latency: capture Synchronous <code>POST /documents</code> returns <code>202</code> under 300 ms Latency: first content Median time to the first <code>section.ready</code> on warm caches under 20 s Latency: full deck Eight-section deck to <code>document.ready</code> on warm caches under 90 s Latency: text brief <code>kind: text</code> with <code>options.sync: true</code> 20 s budget, then falls back to async <code>202</code> Latency: stage budget Data Digest 1 to 10 s, outline 5 to 15 s, resolution 1 to 20 s, narrative 3 to 8 s per section (up to 4 in parallel), export 1 to 10 s per format per stage Scale 20 concurrent deck jobs on 4 DBD workers without starving the default Celery queue load test Scale: per document <code>MAX_BINDINGS_PER_DOC = 60</code>, <code>MAX_ROWS_PER_BINDING = 5000</code>; chart bindings capped at 50 rows, tables at 200 configuration Cost: tokens Data Digest bounded by <code>max_digest_tokens = 12000</code>; outline 1 call, tie-break 0 or 1, writer 1 per section plus at most 1 fact follow-up and 2 repairs per document Cost: agent <code>agent</code> planner budget of 24 tool calls, 6 minutes and 60,000 input tokens per job Cost: queries Profiling uses the dashboard's own queries, so with a configured <code>DATA_CACHE_CONFIG</code> they are usually cache hits; the cache key includes RLS (<code>apache/superset: superset/security/manager.py:6304</code>) per user Determinism: numbers Same binding results produce the same facts, displays, chart caches and table cells, always exact Determinism: layout Layout fit is a deterministic bipartite match plus score; only ties consult the LLM exact except ties Determinism: render Render cache key <code>sha256(spec_sha256, data_hashes, template_version, renderer_version, format)</code> reuses artifacts exact Determinism: prose Not deterministic, but verified: zero untokenized numerals and entities in shipped LLM text acceptance Accuracy 100% of chart, table and KPI values in an exported PPTX equal <code>GET /api/v1/chart/&lt;id&gt;/data/</code> for the same user and filters acceptance Quality Schema-valid outputs at least 99% after retries; Verifier precision at least 0.98 and recall at least 0.95 per language eval suite Auditability Every artifact references <code>(document_id, version, binding_result_ids)</code>, so "what data was in Monday's file" is answerable always Security Two users with different RLS get different, correct numbers from one document, with no cross-leak into artifacts, events or audit rows contract test Compatibility Runs on Superset 6.1.0 with flagged fallbacks and on master; two frontend bundles from one source both Operability No new database server or web service; one wheel in the images, one <code>.supx</code>, one Celery queue, one beat entry deployment Portability Opens in PowerPoint 365, Keynote and LibreOffice with working "Edit Data" acceptance <p>Gotcha: latency targets assume a data cache</p> <p>Superset's default <code>DATA_CACHE_CONFIG</code> is <code>{"CACHE_TYPE": "NullCache"}</code> (<code>apache/superset: superset/config.py:1532</code>), so nothing is cached out of the box. Profiling then re-runs every dashboard query, and the 20 s and 90 s targets become a function of warehouse speed. <code>superset dbd doctor</code> warns when the data cache is <code>NullCache</code>.</p> <p>The accuracy requirement is an executable acceptance test. A sketch, using python-pptx's read API and the chart data endpoint as the same user:</p> tests/acceptance/test_pptx_matches_chart_data.py (sketch)<pre><code>import requests
from pptx import Presentation


def bound_chart_frames(path: str):
    for slide in Presentation(path).slides:
        for shape in slide.shapes:
            if shape.has_chart and shape.name.startswith("dbd:chart:"):
                _, _, element_id, binding_id = shape.name.split(":", 3)
                yield element_id, binding_id, shape.chart


def test_values_equal_chart_data(pptx_path, spec, superset_url, user_session):
    for _element_id, binding_id, chart in bound_chart_frames(pptx_path):
        binding = spec.binding(binding_id)
        if binding.source.kind != "chart":
            continue
        # Same user, same filters: master supports ?filters_dashboard_id=&lt;id&gt;
        res = user_session.get(
            f"{superset_url}/api/v1/chart/{binding.source.chart_id}/data/",
            params={"filters_dashboard_id": spec.context.dashboard.dashboard_id},
        ).json()["result"][binding.source.query_index]
        expected = shape_rows(res, binding.shape)  # test helper: same shaping as the resolver
        for series in chart.plots[0].series:
            assert list(series.values) == expected[series.name]
</code></pre> <p><code>filters_dashboard_id</code> applies only the dashboard's saved filter defaults (it passes no active data mask) and exists only on master (<code>apache/superset: superset/charts/data/api.py:191-219</code>). The comparison is therefore exact only for documents with <code>filter_mode: "defaults"</code>, or for fixture dashboards whose defaults equal the captured selection; a document captured with other filter values (UC1's EMEA selection, for example) is compared against a <code>POST /api/v1/chart/data</code> request built from the same permalink data mask. On 6.1 the test compares against the resolver's own 6.1 filter port, and a contract test asserts that the port and the master helper produce identical query contexts.</p>

Decorator Module What registration does Availability <code>@api(id, name, description=None, resource_name=None)</code> on a <code>RestApi</code> subclass <code>superset_core.rest_api.decorators</code> In extension context the route base becomes <code>/extensions/{publisher}/{name}</code> plus <code>/{resource_name}</code>, the id becomes <code>extensions.{publisher}.{name}.{id}</code>, and the class is registered immediately with <code>appbuilder.add_api</code> and <code>appbuilder._add_permission</code> (<code>apache/superset: superset/core/api/core_api_injection.py:179-227</code>) [6.1] <code>@tool(func_or_name=None, *, name=None, description=None, tags=None, protect=True, class_permission_name=None, method_permission_name=None, annotations=None)</code>, usable bare or called <code>superset_core.mcp.decorators</code> (<code>:51-61</code>) Name prefixed <code>extensions.{publisher}.{name}.{tool}</code>; wrapped in <code>mcp_auth_hook</code> when <code>protect=True</code>; added to the FastMCP singleton (<code>apache/superset: superset/core/mcp/core_mcp_injection.py:145-271</code>) [6.1] <code>@prompt(func_or_name=None, *, name=None, title=None, description=None, tags: set[str] or None=None, protect=True)</code> <code>superset_core.mcp.decorators</code> (<code>:130-138</code>) Same prefixing and auth [6.1] <code>@task(name=None, scope=TaskScope.PRIVATE, timeout=None, subscription_policy=None)</code> <code>superset_core.tasks.decorators</code> (<code>:32-37</code>) Task type prefixed <code>extensions.{publisher}.{name}.</code>; registered in <code>TaskRegistry</code> (<code>apache/superset: superset/tasks/decorators.py:147-175</code>) [6.1]; <code>subscription_policy</code> [M] <code>@semantic_layer(id, name, description=None)</code> <code>superset_core.semantic_layers</code> Registers a semantic layer provider [M], behind <code>SEMANTIC_LAYERS</code> <p>With <code>@api(id="dbd_api", name="Data-Bound Documents API", resource_name="v1")</code> inside <code>databound.docgen</code>, every route lives under <code>/extensions/databound/docgen/v1</code>. That is the REST base path used throughout Part VII. Note it is not under <code>/api/v1</code>.</p> backend/src/databound/docgen/api.py (minimal skeleton)<pre><code>from flask import Response, g
from flask_appbuilder.api import expose, permission_name, protect, safe
from superset_core.rest_api.api import RestApi
from superset_core.rest_api.decorators import api


@api(id="dbd_api", name="Data-Bound Documents API", resource_name="v1",
     description="Generate data-bound decks, documents, pages and briefs")
class DataBoundDocsApi(RestApi):
    class_permission_name = "DataBoundDocs"
    csrf_exempt = False                 # FAB BaseApi defaults to True
    method_permission_name = {"capabilities": "read"}

    @expose("/capabilities", methods=("GET",))
    @protect()
    @safe
    @permission_name("read")
    def capabilities(self) -&gt; Response:
